# Simple, linear code

Apply this principle across projects and languages, including Java and Groovy.

- Prefer straightforward, linear code with important logic visible at the
  point of use.
- Keep short, readable logic inline.
- Extract a method when it removes meaningful duplication, gives a useful name
  to genuinely complex logic, or creates a real independently testable
  boundary.
- Do not create small methods merely to move a few straightforward lines away
  from their caller.
- Avoid additional layers, interfaces, wrappers, and generic abstractions
  unless they solve a current, concrete requirement.
- During design review, remove indirection that makes the execution flow harder
  to follow without providing a clear benefit.
